}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5e/5ed28b1e7b49276cdb59da432056597e5992b8c4015ac8e9384f6bfe3f1fb7ba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2=r^{2} + 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555026} {:answer/id #uuid "24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555073} {:answer/id #uuid "26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555081} {:answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555305} {:answer/id #uuid "acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555305} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" :question/math-academy-id "q-161628" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555089} {:answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555125} {:answer/id #uuid "c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555462} {:answer/id #uuid "f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555596} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555125} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555682}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-161631" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -3}}}^{3}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r({r}^{2}+1)}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137dcd13-d6dd-5100-aa1a-4ef83376e70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564970} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565100} {:answer/id #uuid "8156f8ac-e493-504e-aade-245eb369fa1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565139} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6bc81a-88bf-5b77-b8c7-6ed75d98d631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565174} {:answer/id #uuid "dd82ed50-ec04-508c-a578-c8dd2bbd72c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565100} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d4b2ef-ed57-5651-8502-b9160bd5e70c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565381}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66285a95-784c-5363-bee3-101afc12cafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161635" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le r\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{0}^{r}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c0fc71-469e-590c-ba8b-284e07e9d56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600512} {:answer/id #uuid "49003dc7-d1f6-5706-9ec6-4e81c0fa6b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600563} {:answer/id #uuid "4ada50cc-a17f-524a-b317-1e1efb616136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600567} {:answer/id #uuid "c950dfc3-14f5-582e-8ccb-600b02b54ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{r+1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600660} {:answer/id #uuid "e49f8e80-604e-5e97-939f-664f1887cc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e49f8e80-604e-5e97-939f-664f1887cc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600684} :answer-field/id #uuid "69082d16-ac02-5957-841e-63c1d5f5e644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600738}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce2ff483-7446-5ef4-9532-e3d923aa91d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161634" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0,\\,x\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/27/27a82956e22b5b2e2de2c02d9d93fb7b7ce57a2b581b88458964eda465e8bebd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1={r}^{2}+1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+1}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid"}]}
